  "chapter": 1,
  "title": "The Vocative & Neuter Nouns",
  "subtitle": "Direct address · Introduction to Neuter nouns in -a · The complete case paradigm",
  "tags": [
    "New",
    "Cases",
    "Neuter nouns"
  ],
  "minutes": 15,
  "revision": {
    "title": "Quick Revision — All Seven Cases",
    "summaryHtml": "You've mastered seven cases for masculine <em>-a</em> nouns! This lesson completes the paradigm with the <strong>Vocative</strong> and introduces <strong>Neuter nouns</strong>.",
    "connectionHtml": "<strong>Connection:</strong> The Vocative is the simplest case — the base form itself for singular. Combined with neuter nouns, you'll complete all the foundational patterns of Pāli nouns.",
    "questions": [
      {
        "q": "Name all seven cases you've learned.",
        "a": "Nom, Acc, Ins, Abl, Dat, Gen, Loc"
      },
      {
        "q": "What is the Locative plural ending?",
        "a": "<strong>-esu</strong>"
      },
      {
        "q": "Which cases share the plural ending -ehi?",
        "a": "<strong>Instrumental</strong> and <strong>Ablative</strong>"
      }
    ]
  },
  "teach": [
    {
      "id": "grammar",
      "title": "The Vocative CaseDirect Address",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "eyebrow": "Grammar — Part A",
          "html": "The <em>Vocative</em> Case<br>Direct Address"
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "html": "The Vocative is used for <strong>direct address</strong> — calling to someone. In English: \"O man!\" or just \"Man!\""
        },
        {
          "type": "rule",
          "title": "For masculine nouns ending in -a:",
          "formulas": [
            "Singular: <em>bare base</em> <span>|</span> <em>nara!</em> (O man!)",
            "Plural:&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;base + <em>-ā</em> <span>|</span> <em>narā!</em> (O men!)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "html": "The Vocative singular is just the <strong>uninflected base</strong> — the simplest case! The plural is <strong>-ā</strong> (same as Nominative plural)."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 3,
          "html": "Complete Masculine -a Paradigm"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "caption": "All eight cases — masculine nouns in -a (base: nara)",
          "head": [
            "Case",
            "Function",
            "Singular",
            "Plural"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Nominative",
              "Subject",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">o</span>",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">ā</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Vocative",
              "Address",
              "nara <em>(base)</em>",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">ā</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Accusative",
              "Object",
              "nara<span class=\"decl-ending\">ṃ</span>",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">e</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Instrumental",
              "By/with",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">ena</span>",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">ehi</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Ablative",
              "From",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">ā/asmā/amhā</span>",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">ehi</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Dative",
              "For/to",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">āya/assa</span>",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">ānaṃ</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Genitive",
              "Of",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">assa</span>",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">ānaṃ</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Locative",
              "In/at",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">asmiṃ/e</span>",
              "nar<span class=\"decl-ending\">esu</span>"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "neuter",
      "title": "Neuter Nouns in -a",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "eyebrow": "Grammar — Part B",
          "html": "<em>Neuter</em> Nouns in -a"
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "html": "Neuter nouns follow the <strong>same pattern</strong> as masculine nouns for most cases. They differ only in the <strong>Nominative, Vocative, and Accusative</strong>:"
        },
        {
          "type": "rule",
          "title": "Neuter -a nouns differ in these three cases:",
          "formulas": [
            "Nom/Voc/Acc Singular: base + <em>-aṃ</em> <span>|</span> phala<em>ṃ</em> (fruit)",
            "Nom/Voc/Acc Plural:&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;base + <em>-āni</em> <span>|</span> phal<em>āni</em> (fruits)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "html": "Common neuter nouns: <strong>phala</strong> (fruit), <strong>nayana</strong> (eye), <strong>nagara</strong> (city), <strong>udaka</strong> (water), <strong>citta</strong> (mind)."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "caption": "Neuter -a nouns (base: phala — fruit)",
          "head": [
            "Case",
            "Singular",
            "Plural"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Nom/Voc/Acc",
              "phala<span class=\"decl-ending\">ṃ</span>",
              "phal<span class=\"decl-ending\">āni</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Ins",
              "phal<span class=\"decl-ending\">ena</span>",
              "phal<span class=\"decl-ending\">ehi</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Abl",
              "phal<span class=\"decl-ending\">ā/asmā</span>",
              "phal<span class=\"decl-ending\">ehi</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Dat/Gen",
              "phal<span class=\"decl-ending\">assa</span>",
              "phal<span class=\"decl-ending\">ānaṃ</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Loc",
              "phal<span class=\"decl-ending\">asmiṃ/e</span>",
              "phal<span class=\"decl-ending\">esu</span>"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
